    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The full test suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 0 failures/errors).\n\n## Summary\n\nImplemented the \"no-future-date\" rule: a supplied `registrationDate` later than the server's current date is now rejected with **400**, while a null date (defaults to today) and back-dating remain allowed.\n\nFollowing the project's OfficeFloor function-injection conventions (checked exception + escalation handler YAML, not `@ControllerAdvice`), I:\n\n1. **`FutureRegistrationDateException.java`** \u2014 new checked exception (`extends Exception`), mirroring the existing `InvalidEmailException` pattern.\n2. **`FutureRegistrationDateExceptionHandler.java`** \u2014 responds `400` with `{\"errors\":[\"registrationDate\"]}`, matching the existing 400 handlers.\n3. **`...FutureRegistrationDateException.yml`** \u2014 escalation wiring under `officefloor/escalation/`.\n4. **`ResolveRegistrationDate.java`** \u2014 added the guard: if a date is supplied and `isAfter(LocalDate.now())`, throw the exception (before the weekend business-day roll); declared it in the `throws` clause so it surfaces as an escalation.\n\nThe check lives in `ResolveRegistrationDate` because that step already owns the supplied-vs-default date logic, keeping the future-date rejection consistent with how the effective date is otherwise resolved.",
